%% ---- The reporting pass (llmdetailed §10.5.1) -----------------------------
%%
%% What the derived pack already gives this model without being asked: a
%% register per entity, a breakdown per %%enum-bound column, created-per-month,
%% a lifecycle report over all eleven state machines in the diagram's own order,
%% and children-per-parent for all 137 relationships. None of that needed anyone
%% who has advised a client.
%%
%% These do. Each of the six roles on the %%rbac matrix above is walked through
%% the four questions — what they open first, what tells them something is wrong
%% today, what they are asked for weekly, and what rots silently if nobody
%% looks. The sets here are smaller than the CRM's because these read-lists are:
%% an advisor may reason about three entities, a dealer about one. The test is
%% the role's own job, not a count.

%% -- advisor: the book they are responsible for ------------------------------
    %%report advisor-clients-stuck-onboarding title: Clients who never finished onboarding entity: Client help: The first question of the week. A client in onboarding or pending KYC past a month is revenue that has been sold and not started, and every one of them is waiting on a document somebody forgot to chase. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, c.status, c.onboarding_date, EXTRACT(day FROM now() - c.onboarding_date)::int AS days_since FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status IN ('onboarding', 'pending_kyc') AND c.onboarding_date < now()::date - 30 ORDER BY days_since DESC
    %%report advisor-clients-without-portfolio title: Active clients with no portfolio entity: Client help: An active client with nothing invested is either a sale that stopped halfway or a data error, and both look identical from the client screen. Only the absence of a portfolio shows it. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, c.client_type, c.service_model, c.onboarding_date FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id LEFT JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.household_id = c.household_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' GROUP BY c.id, c.client_number, y.display_name, c.client_type, c.service_model, c.onboarding_date HAVING COUNT(p.id) = 0 ORDER BY c.onboarding_date
    %%report advisor-book-by-service-model title: The book, by service model entity: Client chart: bar x: service_model y: clients help: What an advisor is actually carrying, which is the conversation about capacity. A book that is mostly the highest-touch model is one that cannot take another client, whatever the headcount says. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(c.service_model, ''), '(not set)') AS service_model, COUNT(*) AS clients, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE c.status = 'active') AS active FROM bus_client c WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status NOT IN ('closed', 'inactive') GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY clients DESC
    %%report advisor-dormant-clients title: Active clients nobody has spoken to entity: Client help: The account that rots silently. An active client with no interaction recorded in six months has usually already been approached by somebody else, and the first anyone here hears of it is the transfer request. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, c.status, MAX(i.interaction_at) AS last_contact FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id LEFT JOIN bus_interaction i ON i.party_id = c.party_id AND i.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' GROUP BY c.id, c.client_number, y.display_name, c.status HAVING MAX(i.interaction_at) IS NULL OR MAX(i.interaction_at) < now() - interval '180 days' ORDER BY last_contact NULLS FIRST

%% -- client: what the client themself can see --------------------------------
    %%report client-proposals-awaiting-answer title: Proposals presented and not answered entity: Proposal help: What the client has been asked to decide and has not. Shown with the expiry date, because a proposal that lapses has to be rebuilt at current prices rather than simply re-sent. sql: SELECT p.proposal_number, y.display_name AS client, p.status, p.proposal_date, p.expiry_date, (p.expiry_date - now()::date) AS days_to_expiry FROM bus_proposal p JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = p.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'presented' AND p.expiry_date >= now()::date ORDER BY p.expiry_date
    %%report client-proposals-expired title: Proposals that lapsed unanswered entity: Proposal help: Every one is work already done and thrown away. Read weekly it is a list of calls to make; read never, it is the reason the pipeline number is wrong. sql: SELECT p.proposal_number, y.display_name AS client, p.proposal_date, p.expiry_date FROM bus_proposal p JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = p.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND (p.status = 'expired' OR (p.status = 'presented' AND p.expiry_date < now()::date)) ORDER BY p.expiry_date DESC
    %%report client-portfolios-by-objective title: Portfolios by stated objective entity: Portfolio chart: pie x: objective y: portfolios help: What the client asked the money to do, in their own words rather than the risk model's. Where this and the risk profile disagree, the suitability file is the thing that has to explain it. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(p.investment_objective, ''), '(not stated)') AS objective, COUNT(*) AS portfolios FROM bus_portfolio p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY portfolios DESC

%% -- dealer: the portfolios they trade ---------------------------------------
    %%report dealer-portfolios-without-risk-profile title: Portfolios with no risk profile attached entity: Portfolio help: A portfolio nobody can check a trade against. The dealing desk cannot assess suitability without it, so the trade either waits or goes through unchecked — and the second is the one that becomes a complaint. sql: SELECT p.portfolio_name, p.portfolio_type, p.base_currency, p.investment_objective, p.status FROM bus_portfolio p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND p.risk_profile_id IS NULL ORDER BY p.portfolio_name
    %%report dealer-portfolios-by-currency title: Live portfolios by base currency entity: Portfolio chart: bar x: currency y: portfolios help: What the desk has to be able to settle in. A currency with a handful of portfolios still needs the same custody arrangement as the main one, and that cost is invisible until somebody counts. sql: SELECT p.base_currency AS currency, p.portfolio_type, COUNT(*) AS portfolios FROM bus_portfolio p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' GROUP BY p.base_currency, p.portfolio_type ORDER BY portfolios DESC

%% -- compliance_officer: the file that has to be defensible ------------------
    %%report compliance-assessments-overdue title: Clients with no current suitability assessment entity: SuitabilityAssessment help: The single finding a regulator looks for. An active client whose most recent assessment is over a year old is being advised on a view of their circumstances that is out of date, and nothing prompts anyone. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, MAX(s.assessment_date) AS last_assessed, (now()::date - MAX(s.assessment_date)) AS days_since FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id LEFT JOIN bus_suitability_assessment s ON s.client_id = c.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'approved' WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' GROUP BY c.id, c.client_number, y.display_name HAVING MAX(s.assessment_date) IS NULL OR MAX(s.assessment_date) < now()::date - 365 ORDER BY last_assessed NULLS FIRST
    %%report compliance-assessments-unapproved title: Assessments completed but never approved entity: SuitabilityAssessment help: The assessment was done and then stalled before sign-off, so the client counts as unassessed however much work went in. It is the cheapest compliance gap in the system to close. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, s.assessment_type, s.status, s.assessment_date, s.result FROM bus_suitability_assessment s JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = s.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('draft', 'pending') AND s.assessment_date < now()::date - 14 ORDER BY s.assessment_date
    %%report compliance-overrides title: Records changed outside the normal path entity: AuditEvent help: The audit trail read the way an investigator reads it: not everything that happened, but the actions that overrode, cancelled or superseded something. These are the entries that need a reason recorded against them. sql: SELECT a."timestamp" AS occurred_at, y.display_name AS advisor, a.action, a.entity_type, a.entity_ref, a.source FROM bus_audit_event a LEFT JOIN bus_advisor v ON v.id = a.advisor_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = v.party_id WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.action ILIKE ANY (ARRAY['%override%', '%cancel%', '%supersede%', '%delete%', '%reject%']) AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '90 days' ORDER BY a."timestamp" DESC
    %%report compliance-activity-by-advisor title: Audited actions by advisor entity: AuditEvent chart: bar x: advisor y: actions help: Not a productivity measure and must not be read as one. It is the denominator: an advisor with far more audited changes than the rest is either carrying more, or working around something. sql: SELECT COALESCE(y.display_name, '(system)') AS advisor, COUNT(*) AS actions, COUNT(DISTINCT a.entity_type) AS entity_types FROM bus_audit_event a LEFT JOIN bus_advisor v ON v.id = a.advisor_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = v.party_id WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '30 days' GROUP BY y.display_name ORDER BY actions DESC

%% -- finance_user: what has been earned and what has landed ------------------
    %%report finance-commission-outstanding title: Commission earned and not settled entity: Commission chart: bar x: commission_type y: amount help: What is owed to the firm, by the kind of business that generated it. Anything still unsettled after a quarter is a reconciliation nobody finished, not a payment that is late. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(m.commission_type, ''), '(not set)') AS commission_type, COUNT(*) AS entries, SUM(m.amount) AS amount FROM bus_commission m WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status IN ('pending', 'active') GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY amount DESC
    %%report finance-commission-by-month title: Commission earned by month entity: Commission chart: line x: month y: amount help: The revenue line the business is run on. Earned date rather than received, because that is the month the work belongs to however long the settlement takes. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', m.earned_date), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, SUM(m.amount) AS amount, COUNT(*) AS entries FROM bus_commission m WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.earned_date > now()::date - 365 GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report finance-commission-failures title: Commission entries in exception entity: Commission help: Entries the reconciliation could not match. Each is money the firm believes it earned and the counterparty does not, and every one has to be chased individually before it ages out. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, m.commission_type, m.amount, m.earned_date, m.status FROM bus_commission m LEFT JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = m.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status IN ('failed', 'rejected', 'cancelled') ORDER BY m.earned_date DESC

%% -- administrator: what nobody else can see ---------------------------------
    %%report admin-audit-gaps title: Days with no audit activity at all entity: AuditEvent help: A silent day in an audit trail is either a genuinely quiet day or a collector that stopped writing. The difference matters enormously and only shows up when the days are counted rather than the events. sql: SELECT to_char(d.day, 'YYYY-MM-DD') AS day, COUNT(a.id) AS events FROM generate_series((now() - interval '30 days')::date, now()::date, interval '1 day') AS d(day) LEFT JOIN bus_audit_event a ON a."timestamp" >= d.day AND a."timestamp" < d.day + interval '1 day' AND a.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY d.day HAVING COUNT(a.id) = 0 ORDER BY d.day
    %%report admin-actions-without-advisor title: Audited changes with no advisor attached entity: AuditEvent help: An action nobody is recorded as having taken. Some are genuinely the system; the rest are a session that lost its user, and only an administrator can tell the two apart. sql: SELECT a."timestamp" AS occurred_at, a.action, a.entity_type, a.entity_ref, a.source, a.ip_address FROM bus_audit_event a WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.advisor_id IS NULL AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '30 days' ORDER BY a."timestamp" DESC
    %%report admin-audit-by-source title: Where audited changes come from entity: AuditEvent chart: pie x: source y: events help: The mix of interactive, batch and integration traffic. A source that suddenly dominates is usually an integration retrying, and it will fill the audit store long before anybody notices the cost. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(a.source, ''), '(not recorded)') AS source, COUNT(*) AS events FROM bus_audit_event a WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '30 days' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY events DESC

%% ---- Section 20: Workflow — client onboarding (saga) -----------------------
%% Runs when an onboarding case is opened: stands up the household record, the
%% financial profile and the risk profile the rest of the platform reads, then
%% moves the case on. Each step is a row somewhere else, so the flow is a saga
%% rather than a hook.
%%meta name: Client Onboarding
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow ClientOnboarding entity: OnboardingCase kind: saga trigger: automatic operation: INSERT
flowchart TD
    A([Onboarding case opened]) --> B{Grade the household}
    B --> C[Open the household]
    C --> D[Open the risk profile]
    D --> E[Open the financial plan]
    E --> F[Move the case to KYC]
    F --> G([Ready for KYC])

    %%step B Decision decisionTable: {"hitPolicy":"first","inputs":[{"id":"i1","name":"Investable","field":"estimated_aum"}],"outputs":[{"id":"o1","name":"Segment","field":"householdSegment"},{"id":"o2","name":"RiskBand","field":"openingRiskBand"}],"rules":[{"_id":"uhnw","i1":">= 5000000","o1":"'ultra_high_net_worth'","o2":"'moderately_aggressive'"},{"_id":"hnw","i1":">= 1000000","o1":"'high_net_worth'","o2":"'moderate'"},{"_id":"affluent","i1":">= 250000","o1":"'affluent'","o2":"'moderately_conservative'"},{"_id":"rest","i1":"","o1":"'retail'","o2":"'conservative'"}]}
    %%step C CreateEntity entity: Household as: newHouseholdId fields: {"name":"case_reference","status":"active","advisor_id":"advisor_id"}
    %%step D CreateEntity entity: RiskProfile as: newRiskProfileId fields: {"household_id":"newHouseholdId","risk_category":"openingRiskBand","status":"draft"}
    %%step E CreateEntity entity: FinancialPlan as: newFinancialPlanId fields: {"household_id":"newHouseholdId","advisor_id":"advisor_id","status":"draft"}
    %%step F UpdateEntity field: status value: kyc

%% ---- Section 21: Workflow — recommendation execution (saga) ----------------
%% What happens after a client accepts: the acceptance is what starts it, and it
%% ends with an order on the dealing desk and an audit row that says who did it.
%%meta name: Recommendation Execution
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow RecommendationExecution entity: ClientAcceptance kind: saga trigger: automatic operation: INSERT
flowchart TD
    A([Client accepted the proposal]) --> B{Size the first order}
    B --> C[Raise the order]
    C --> D[Write the audit record]
    D --> E[Close the acceptance]
    E --> F([Order with the desk])

    %%step B Decision decisionTable: {"hitPolicy":"first","inputs":[{"id":"i1","name":"Accepted","field":"accepted_amount"}],"outputs":[{"id":"o1","name":"OrderType","field":"openingOrderType"},{"id":"o2","name":"OrderAmount","field":"openingOrderAmount"}],"rules":[{"_id":"block","i1":">= 1000000","o1":"'block'","o2":"accepted_amount"},{"_id":"standard","i1":"","o1":"'market'","o2":"accepted_amount"}]}
    %%step C CreateEntity entity: Order as: newOrderId fields: {"client_id":"client_id","order_type":"openingOrderType","amount":"openingOrderAmount","status":"draft"}
    %%step D CreateEntity entity: AuditEvent as: newAuditEventId fields: {"action":"recommendation_executed","entity_type":"ClientAcceptance","entity_ref":"id","source":"saga"}
    %%step E UpdateEntity field: status value: accepted

%% ---- Section 22: Workflow — rebalancing (saga) -----------------------------
%% Drift is detected against the model portfolio; this is what the platform does
%% about it without waiting for anybody to open a screen.
%%meta name: Rebalancing
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow Rebalancing entity: RebalanceRecommendation kind: saga trigger: automatic operation: INSERT
flowchart TD
    A([Drift detected]) --> B{Grade the drift}
    B --> C[Size the corrective trade]
    C --> D[Write the audit record]
    D --> E[Move the rebalance to analysed]
    E --> F([Awaiting approval])

    %%step B Decision decisionTable: {"hitPolicy":"first","inputs":[{"id":"i1","name":"Deviation","field":"deviation_amount"}],"outputs":[{"id":"o1","name":"Urgency","field":"rebalanceUrgency"},{"id":"o2","name":"Factor","field":"correctionFactor"}],"rules":[{"_id":"urgent","i1":">= 100000","o1":"'priority'","o2":"1"},{"_id":"routine","i1":"","o1":"'routine'","o2":"0.5"}]}
    %%step C Formula target: correctiveTradeAmount operation: multiply source: deviation_amount operand: correctionFactor
    %%step D CreateEntity entity: AuditEvent as: newAuditEventId fields: {"action":"rebalance_detected","entity_type":"RebalanceRecommendation","entity_ref":"id","source":"saga"}
    %%step E UpdateEntity field: status value: analysed
